WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.851
In this video, you will put SoCal Lights on your phone like an app, and see what still works when you have no signal, which happens a lot when you are driving around looking at lights.

00:00:10.754 --> 00:00:14.980
On your phone, open your web browser, and go to SoCal Lights dot app.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.429
This works on any phone.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:18.977
This is the home page.

00:00:18.977 --> 00:00:21.789
There is nothing to download from an app store.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:27.763
You add the site to your Home Screen, and it opens like any other app, full screen, with its own icon.

00:00:28.666 --> 00:00:30.269
On an iPhone, use Safari.

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:33.229
Tap the Share button, the square with an arrow.

00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:35.758
Scroll down, and tap Add to Home Screen.

00:00:35.758 --> 00:00:36.559
Then tap Add.

00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:39.994
On an Android phone, use Chrome.

00:00:39.994 --> 00:00:43.935
Tap the three dots, then tap Install app, or Add to Home screen.

00:00:45.338 --> 00:00:46.530
Now the useful part.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:52.204
With no signal, the app still opens, because the site saves your last list of places on your phone.

00:00:52.204 --> 00:00:59.013
So when you are out looking at lights, in a part of town with weak coverage, you can still see what you were looking at.

00:00:59.914 --> 00:01:04.373
When your signal comes back, the app updates by itself, with the latest events.

00:01:04.373 --> 00:01:06.101
You do not have to do anything.

00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:08.851
Next up: Find contests.
